// Expected: c0c0 at one session and at two.
//
// Password extraction asks whether the attacker can verify a guess offline.
// The password here is only ever used through PW_HASH, which declares its
// first position as password-hashing, so the cascade stops there and the
// password is not recoverable however many transcripts the attacker collects.
//
// Replication is exactly what would tempt a wrong answer: two sessions mean
// two salts and two ciphertexts drawn from one password, which is the shape
// of a real offline attack and is the sort of thing a symbolic tool can talk
// itself into. It must not. Both queries hold at both session counts, and if
// this model ever starts reporting c1 the place to look is whether a
// per-session copy of the salt lost the qualifier that keeps it inside the
// password-hashing position.

attacker[active]

principal Alice[
	knows password pw
	generates salt_n
	derived_a = PW_HASH(pw, salt_n)
	generates m
	e = ENC(derived_a, m)
]

Alice -> Bob: salt_n, e

principal Bob[
	knows password pw
	derived_b = PW_HASH(pw, salt_n)
	d = DEC(derived_b, e)
]

queries[
	confidentiality? pw
	confidentiality? m
]
